  <h1>Post-Quantum Cryptography</h1>
  <p class="lead">P31 implements all three NIST FIPS standards for post-quantum cryptography, ensuring long-term security against quantum computer attacks.</p>

  <h2>NIST Standards</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Algorithm</th>
        <th>FIPS</th>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th>Key Size</th>
        <th>Signature Size</th>
        <th>Use Case</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>ML-KEM-768</strong></td>
        <td>FIPS 203</td>
        <td>KEM</td>
        <td>1184 / 2400 bytes</td>
        <td>1088 bytes</td>
        <td>Secure key exchange</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>ML-DSA-65</strong></td>
        <td>FIPS 204</td>
        <td>Signature</td>
        <td>1952 / 4032 bytes</td>
        <td>3309 bytes</td>
        <td>Document signing, DID</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>SLH-DSA-128s</strong></td>
        <td>FIPS 205</td>
        <td>Signature</td>
        <td>32 / 64 bytes</td>
        <td>7856 bytes</td>
        <td>Long-term archival</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Triple-Signature Credentials</h2>
  <p>P31 Cognitive Passports use triple-signature credentials for maximum security:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Ed25519</strong> — Classical signature (fast, widely supported)</li>
    <li><strong>ML-DSA-65</strong> — Post-quantum lattice-based signature</li>
    <li><strong>SLH-DSA-128s</strong> — Hash-based signature for long-term security</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout callout-info">
    <div class="callout-title">Defense in Depth</div>
    <p>Triple signatures ensure that even if one algorithm is compromised, the credential remains valid. This provides security against both classical and quantum adversaries.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Usage in Workers</h2>
  <pre><code>import {'{'} ml_kem768 {'}'} from '@noble/post-quantum/ml-kem.js';
import {'{'} ml_dsa65 {'}'} from '@noble/post-quantum/ml-dsa.js';

// Key encapsulation
const kp = ml_kem768.keygen();
const {'{'} cipherText, sharedSecret {'}'} = ml_kem768.encapsulate(kp.publicKey);
const decapped = ml_kem768.decapsulate(cipherText, kp.secretKey);

// Digital signature
const msg = new TextEncoder().encode('document');
const sig = ml_dsa65.sign(msg, kp.secretKey);
const valid = ml_dsa65.verify(sig, msg, kp.publicKey);</code></pre>

  <h2>DID Integration</h2>
  <p>P31 DIDs support multiple key types:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>did:key</strong> — Ed25519 (primary, Web Crypto)</li>
    <li><strong>did:jwk</strong> — ML-DSA-65 (encoded as <code>kty:AKP</code>, RFC 9964)</li>
    <li><strong>did:web</strong> — HTTPS domain resolution (DID Core v1.1 §8.3)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Migration Path</h2>
  <p>The P31 stack supports hybrid classical + post-quantum cryptography during the transition period:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Ed25519 signatures remain valid</li>
    <li>ML-DSA-65 co-signatures are added incrementally</li>
    <li>SLH-DSA-128s used for archival and high-value credentials</li>
    <li>Full quantum-safe migration by 2030 (NIST timeline)</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Testing</h2>
  <p>PQC operations are tested with Vitest. Run the test suite:</p>
  <pre><code>cd production/shell
pnpm test src/__tests__/pqc-eudi.test.ts</code></pre>

